00:32:32,970 --> 00:32:35,316
Rym: And I'm looking like, what 4K content is really out there?

371
00:32:35,596 --> 00:32:41,750
Rym: And I found a channel that has that actually has some 8K content since YouTube.

372
00:32:42,590 --> 00:32:50,950
Scott: YouTube is absolutely loaded with HDRs, 4Ks, 8Ks, high refresh rates, because really, it's the only platform that has supports all those things properly.

373
00:32:52,770 --> 00:32:59,924
Rym: But most of the 8K content I could find was really basically nothing but ads.

374
00:33:00,365 --> 00:33:02,128
Rym: Like, it wasn't actually like real content.

375
00:33:02,148 --> 00:33:03,090
Rym: It wasn't anything interesting.

376
00:33:03,410 --> 00:33:05,790
Scott: It's usually like video of animals and shit like that.

377
00:33:06,150 --> 00:33:06,628
Rym: Pure tech demos.

378
00:33:07,210 --> 00:33:08,408
Scott: Landscapes, you know.

379
00:33:08,751 --> 00:33:08,911
Rym: Yeah.

380
00:33:08,931 --> 00:33:15,770
Rym: Well, speaking of which, so the one I found that was actually pretty good and I actually did enjoy watching this video, like in the background while I was doing some other stuff.

381
00:33:15,830 --> 00:33:20,585
Scott: See, that's rare, because usually the demo videos are just like these, you know, makes you feel like you're in a Best Buy.

382
00:33:20,605 --> 00:33:22,250
Scott: There's no entertainment value to the head.

383
00:33:22,890 --> 00:33:30,870
Rym: This video is called Small Birds, because birds has all caps, 8K Ultra HD with names and sounds.

384
00:33:31,571 --> 00:33:32,843
Scott: So they're not HDR, though?

385
00:33:32,864 --> 00:33:33,288
Scott: They're just 8K?

386
00:33:33,796 --> 00:33:34,268
Rym: They're just 8K.

387
00:33:35,710 --> 00:33:38,255
Rym: It is just video.

388
00:33:38,515 --> 00:33:47,350
Rym: Very high quality close-up video of cool birds doing their thing, and it tells you what each bird is in case you didn't recognize them, and it's only 20 minutes long.

389
00:33:47,850 --> 00:33:49,557
Rym: It's actually the kind of content I like.

390
00:33:49,818 --> 00:33:50,561
Rym: I like birds.

391
00:33:50,582 --> 00:33:51,485
Rym: I like looking at birds.

392
00:33:51,566 --> 00:33:52,570
Rym: Bird watching is a cool hobby.

393
00:33:53,630 --> 00:34:03,850
Rym: And also, if you have somehow an 8K monitor out there and you want to watch something that's actually 8K, this is one of the few things out there that isn't just a Best Buy, like, eye assault.

394
00:34:06,354 --> 00:34:06,948
Rym: So what do you got?

395
00:34:09,094 --> 00:34:10,549
Scott: What was my thing of the day?

396
00:34:11,251 --> 00:34:14,090
Rym: Your thing of the day was the arcade cabinet thing.

397
00:34:14,449 --> 00:34:14,911
Scott: Oh, right.

398
00:34:15,012 --> 00:34:19,183
Scott: So, you know, nowadays it's pretty rare for there to be, like, an arcade.

399
00:34:19,223 --> 00:34:21,829
Scott: that's a permanent installation that lots of people go to.

400
00:34:22,871 --> 00:34:25,830
Scott: They're just in certain places in the world, usually amusement areas.

401
00:34:26,713 --> 00:34:27,902
Scott: And even those are not great.

402
00:34:27,942 --> 00:34:28,969
Scott: They're usually full of cranes.

403
00:34:29,692 --> 00:34:36,369
Scott: And the actual arcade machines of the world are usually in someone's house, in a collector's warehouse, at a magfest, right?

404
00:34:36,969 --> 00:34:45,130
Scott: And those are sort of these temporary, either, you know, setups where, like, you know, in order to play, the game's got to be moved and set up, right?

405
00:34:45,690 --> 00:34:48,762
Scott: And moving arcade cabinets really sucks.

406
00:34:48,822 --> 00:34:51,090
Scott: They're, you know, the classic ones are big.

407
00:34:51,630 --> 00:34:53,797
Scott: And, you know, you can't really take them apart.

408
00:34:53,819 --> 00:34:55,203
Scott: There's a big CRT in there.

409
00:34:56,206 --> 00:34:56,989
Scott: It's not a great deal.

410
00:34:58,191 --> 00:35:04,749
Scott: And so when someone rents a truck to bring their games to magfest from their big warehouse, they can only pick and choose a few, right?

411
00:35:05,410 --> 00:35:07,660
Scott: You know, if they bring a Frogger, they can't bring a millipede.

412
00:35:07,700 --> 00:35:08,745
Scott: That's just how it goes.

413
00:35:08,765 --> 00:35:09,789
Scott: There's only so much room in there.

414
00:35:10,690 --> 00:35:20,690
Scott: These people had the job to make some arcade cabinets for some demo games, for some shows, and possibly some other shows, right?

415
00:35:20,750 --> 00:35:27,810
Scott: That would be reused at various, you know, conferences, conventions, you know, whatever, trade shows around.

416
00:35:28,471 --> 00:35:40,889
Scott: And they said, "All right, we want to make full-size arcade cabinets, but we want them to be able to collapse down and be smaller so that we can then ship a lot of them, or ship them more easily in a smaller

417
00:35:40,929 --> 00:35:41,370
Scott: vehicle.".

418
00:35:41,852 --> 00:35:44,630
Rym: I'd say that increases the complexity by an order of magnitude.

419
00:35:45,491 --> 00:35:51,028
Scott: And then set them up again, and, you know, and it's like, "Hey, that's a great idea,

420
00:35:51,109 --> 00:35:51,470
Scott: actually.".

421
00:35:51,510 --> 00:36:05,170
Scott: Because then someone with just like a van instead of a giant box truck could bring, you know, many arcade games to a convention instead of just a few for a lot less, you know, time, effort, money.

422
00:36:06,551 --> 00:36:09,056
Scott: And so they succeeded in doing exactly that.

423
00:36:09,096 --> 00:36:13,565
Scott: They designed and manufactured some collapsible arcade cabinets.

424
00:36:13,665 --> 00:36:16,290
Scott: Obviously not having a CRT helps a lot.

425
00:36:16,850 --> 00:36:22,410
Scott: Obviously not having a whole bunch of old boards and just having modern chips in there helps a lot.

426
00:36:23,671 --> 00:36:28,903
Scott: But even so, it's still, the cabinet itself is still like, you know, full-size.

427
00:36:28,943 --> 00:36:32,030
Scott: It's not like one of those tiny ones you can buy for, you know, your house or whatever.

428
00:36:34,031 --> 00:36:35,072
Scott: So, really good design.

429
00:36:35,874 --> 00:36:45,870
Scott: And I hope people can, you know, if I were to make an arcade cabinet to, say, bring to a magfest with a game that I made in it, I would probably just get this design and make one myself.

430
00:36:46,030 --> 00:36:49,909
Rym: I mean, a big part of that is, I imagine, you'd want to put that in my trunk and then I...

431
00:36:50,010 --> 00:36:56,987
Scott: Yeah, we could just bring it in the trunk, one of them, in the trunk of a car instead of having to rent a vehicle that is capable of hauling.

432
00:36:57,268 --> 00:36:58,290
Scott: That would be kind of awesome.

433
00:36:58,832 --> 00:36:59,152
Rym: Yeah.

434
00:36:59,975 --> 00:37:05,310
Rym: If I had more space, I absolutely would make a cocktail format cabinet and just have it in my house.

435
00:37:05,350 --> 00:37:07,781
Scott: If I had another room in my house, there would be either...

436
00:37:09,690 --> 00:37:15,090
Rym: I would have one Rampart machine, one Dance Dance Revolution machine, and one...

437
00:37:15,090 --> 00:37:15,728
Scott: That's a big room.

438
00:37:16,110 --> 00:37:19,030
Rym: And one cocktail cabinet if I were rich enough to have space for all three.

439
00:37:19,090 --> 00:37:19,392
Scott: Cocktail.

440
00:37:19,775 --> 00:37:21,890
Scott: I'm not too big a fan of the cocktail ones, honestly.

441
00:37:22,631 --> 00:37:24,610
Rym: Honestly, I like the aesthetic of the cocktail.

442
00:37:24,751 --> 00:37:24,987
Rym: Yeah, oh yes.

443
00:37:25,071 --> 00:37:26,089
Scott: I would use it as a coffee table.

444
00:37:26,832 --> 00:37:28,517
Scott: Yeah.

445
00:37:28,678 --> 00:37:32,690
Scott: But, you know, if I had enough room, obviously the first priority would be air hockey.

446
00:37:33,530 --> 00:37:34,109
Rym: Yeah, yeah.

447
00:37:34,290 --> 00:37:44,930
Scott: The second priority would be a pinball, a real one, a good one, and then I would get an arcade cabinet and, of course, it would just have every ROM ever with me in there.

448
00:37:46,011 --> 00:37:53,290
Scott: I wouldn't pick one game, but I would put into the control panel all the necessary buttons for, like, every game.

449
00:37:53,510 --> 00:37:58,310
Scott: You know, three trackballs, three joysticks, and I guess I'd do four joysticks and I'd play X-Men.

450
00:37:58,370 --> 00:37:59,096
Scott: I wouldn't go to six.

451
00:37:59,116 --> 00:37:59,681
Scott: That's too many.

452
00:38:00,468 --> 00:38:00,710
Scott: Anyway.

453
00:38:01,473 --> 00:38:01,603
Scott: So.

454
00:38:01,932 --> 00:38:02,670
Scott: No steering wheels.

455
00:38:03,270 --> 00:38:03,451
Rym: Yeah.

456
00:38:04,095 --> 00:38:06,570
Rym: Let's get right into it because I think we were long enough on the news there.

457
00:38:07,573 --> 00:38:12,069
Rym: So, we drove somewhere recently in my car and Scott got to watch a delicate dance.

458
00:38:14,190 --> 00:38:34,230
Rym: So, modern cars are increasingly putting, we're not talking about anything that drives the car itself directly, none of the, like, controls of the car, but what used to just be a dashboard and a radio, maybe if you were rich in the olden days, like a CD player or a CD changer, like an 8-track.

459
00:38:34,331 --> 00:38:36,549
Scott: Or you swap out and you put a fancy radio in.

460
00:38:36,670 --> 00:38:36,735
Rym: Yep.

461
00:38:37,410 --> 00:38:39,649
Rym: But you basically had instruments for the car.

462
00:38:40,091 --> 00:38:54,370
Rym: Then you had the entertainment console, and then you had the heating and cooling console, and they're all kind of in the dashboard, and they were largely uniform and pretty standard for a long time, and they were all extremely dumb, often mechanical devices.

463
00:38:54,991 --> 00:38:55,503
Scott: Until very recently.

464
00:38:56,070 --> 00:39:08,863
Scott: A standard set of symbols, even though each car would be different, and some cars had an up-down lever for temperature where others had dials, but it was still clearly hot, cold, red, blue.

465
00:39:09,423 --> 00:39:16,430
Scott: You'd have the same symbol for a rear washer wiper, or a front wiper, or a defroster.

466
00:39:16,650 --> 00:39:23,950
Scott: They would all have the same symbols, car to car to car, and you could figure it out even if you got to rent a car without having to go to the manual, probably.

467
00:39:24,811 --> 00:39:32,950
Rym: But there was a certain sort of UX of these, partly because of the history of where these instruments came out of, but the UX had to be, you know, like Scott described.

468
00:39:33,830 --> 00:39:39,758
Rym: It has to be intuitive to someone who sits down in any car, anywhere in the world.

469
00:39:40,219 --> 00:39:48,290
Rym: Doesn't matter where you are, the car has to be intuitive enough where you can look at the instruments and immediately or very quickly understand how to use them.

470
00:39:48,991 --> 00:39:49,151
Scott: Yup.

471
00:39:49,371 --> 00:39:51,434
Scott: Compare that to, say, an airplane, right?

472
00:39:51,494 --> 00:40:03,750
Scott: Especially a commercial one, where pilots need to have hours and hours and hours of simulator training, and like, you know, on a particular plane to learn the controls of that plane, because it's not the same as the other plane.

473
00:40:03,930 --> 00:40:04,391
Scott: Yup.

474
00:40:04,471 --> 00:40:14,390
Scott: And they have to relearn, and sure, there are a lot of things that are the same plane to plane to plane, because it's still the same core concepts, you know, you still have a stick that goes back and forth.

475
00:40:15,350 --> 00:40:20,081
Scott: But, you know, there's a million, you've seen inside a plane, I'm guessing, and a million switches in there.

476
00:40:20,542 --> 00:40:22,246
Scott: It's like, you got to know what all those do.

477
00:40:22,266 --> 00:40:23,329
Rym: For most of them.

478
00:40:24,130 --> 00:40:31,197
Rym: These constraints in a pre-digital era led to these systems being very simple and having very refined user experiences.

479
00:40:31,918 --> 00:40:43,210
Rym: And the most complex part of any car was the entertainment system in the era where you started to be able to replace them, and you started to be able to try to plug, like, iPods and things into them.

480
00:40:44,330 --> 00:40:47,574
Rym: Ox cables started to appear, and some people put in the fancy ones.

481
00:40:47,915 --> 00:41:00,970
Rym: But now, we've entered an era to where most cars, and we're going to get to a point to where this is all cars, the entertainment system area is replaced by a touchscreen and a shitty computer.

482
00:41:02,851 --> 00:41:06,122
Rym: And these shitty computers are extremely shitty.

483
00:41:06,664 --> 00:41:08,290
Rym: So, we have a Toyota car.

484
00:41:08,310 --> 00:41:13,190
Scott: Not only is the computer itself that is shitty, it is the software for it is shitty.

485
00:41:13,310 --> 00:41:18,510
Rym: Yeah, it's not like they just put Windows on these things, or Android, or something that people- Even if they did, that would be a bad idea.

486
00:41:18,731 --> 00:41:19,249
Rym: Yes, it would.

487
00:41:20,132 --> 00:41:22,290
Scott: I think some of them are Android-based, I don't know exactly.

488
00:41:22,370 --> 00:41:26,024
Rym: I mean, there was the Android-based stuff, but it kind of disappeared, and every car I get in is different.

489
00:41:26,064 --> 00:41:27,450
Rym: It's usually by manufacturer.

490
00:41:28,270 --> 00:41:33,350
Scott: It's weird that they're all different, but yet, once I connect, it's the same.

491
00:41:34,431 --> 00:41:37,238
Rym: Well, because they don't use 90% of the stuff in there, because it's all stupid.

492
00:41:37,459 --> 00:41:38,261
Rym: I think that's the key.

493
00:41:38,702 --> 00:41:41,490
Rym: Almost everything that you can do in these screens is stupid.

494
00:41:43,290 --> 00:41:46,275
Rym: And there's a very small number of things people actually use them for.

495
00:41:46,715 --> 00:41:56,970
Rym: Usually, it's playing music, navigating, and if it's a bad car, also controlling the HVAC, if they don't have the HVAC, actually still having separate physical controls, which thankfully, our car has.

496
00:41:58,191 --> 00:42:12,510
Rym: So, our Toyota, the screen with the bad computer in it, is widely regarded to be the best of the car computers right now, based on my research, in that screen- Even better than if you get a Benz, or a BMW, or something?

497
00:42:13,332 --> 00:42:16,309
Rym: From what I've read, they are worse in many ways.

498
00:42:17,031 --> 00:42:17,930
Scott: Interesting, okay.

499
00:42:18,050 --> 00:42:21,130
Rym: But again, this is me reading Reddit, because I was annoyed about a problem I had.

500
00:42:21,250 --> 00:42:21,891
Scott: Yeah, I think that's.

501
00:42:21,951 --> 00:42:26,697
Scott: another problem, is how many people have experience with lots of these systems in different cars.

502
00:42:27,118 --> 00:42:29,221
Scott: Usually, car reviewers are reviewing the car.

503
00:42:29,421 --> 00:42:36,430
Scott: There might be someone out there, because I don't read this sort of thing, who's reviewing car computers, but I can't imagine there's that many.

504
00:42:37,051 --> 00:42:45,990
Scott: And when you're buying a car, it's like, if you're a car person who has to buy a car, that's probably not at the most important thing for most buyers, right?

505
00:42:46,291 --> 00:42:47,072
Rym: That was near the bottom.

506
00:42:47,112 --> 00:42:51,241
Rym: We didn't even look at what that thing was capable of when we bought this car, because we literally didn't care.

507
00:42:51,541 --> 00:42:55,690
Rym: We needed a very specific kind of car, and all those extra details were just whatever.

508
00:42:58,710 --> 00:43:05,659
Rym: So, Toyota seems to have the most reliable of them, but even then, our car, one day, not too long ago, this car is brand new.

509
00:43:05,719 --> 00:43:07,861
Rym: Like, it's got under 5,000 miles on it.

510
00:43:08,162 --> 00:43:14,710
Rym: It's the newest software, it's the newest screen, like, it's basically the best you're gonna get in a Toyota.

511
00:43:15,371 --> 00:43:24,023
Rym: And all the Toyota's the same, and I've driven a lot of cars of a lot of different manufacturers and experienced their car computers, because until we bought this car, I rented cars.

512
00:43:24,083 --> 00:43:25,125
Rym: anytime I needed a car.

513
00:43:25,485 --> 00:43:28,730
Rym: So, every time I rent a car, it's a random car, and I got to try it out.

514
00:43:30,390 --> 00:43:38,407
Rym: And one day, we get in the car, we start to drive, and the screen just reboots to black, and then clearly just reboots and starts over.

515
00:43:39,088 --> 00:43:39,910
Rym: And it started rebooting.

516
00:43:40,170 --> 00:43:42,469
Scott: Thankfully, that is not involved in the driving part at all.

517
00:43:42,710 --> 00:43:55,190
Rym: Nope, but it meant, now, instead of navigating where the map and the directions are just being done by the car, now I've got to, like, make my phone loud enough to hear it tell me where to turn, because I can't use this computer screen anymore.

518
00:43:56,091 --> 00:44:00,570
Rym: And the other annoying thing is I can't play music, because every time it reboots, everything stops, even the radio.

519
00:44:01,991 --> 00:44:11,930
Rym: And three, every time it reboots, the screen turns on again, bright white, and blinds me if I'm driving at night, like every 30 seconds in a cycle.

520
00:44:13,091 --> 00:44:14,213
Rym: This was extremely annoying.

521
00:44:14,614 --> 00:44:21,390
Rym: So, we stopped at a rest stop to eat some lunch, and I've kind of given up on this because I can't get it to work, and I go Google it around, and I end up on some Reddits.

522
00:44:21,930 --> 00:44:33,770
Rym: And all I find are people who have the exact same problem with random specific Toyota cars, and the answer is always, I don't know, never fixed it, just gave up, disabled the screen.

523
00:44:34,491 --> 00:44:42,150
Rym: Or, if the car was still under warranty, dropped it off at the dealership and it took them three weeks, and I assume they just physically replaced the entire thing.

524
00:44:42,870 --> 00:44:44,853
Scott: Yep, I mean, that's the real problem, right?

525
00:44:44,914 --> 00:44:54,670
Scott: It's like, it's not an open system that you could, say, repair yourself if you were a computer professional, or even if you took it to a computer professional, they couldn't do it, because it's a closed system.

526
00:44:55,150 --> 00:45:00,676
Scott: And then, if you take it to the dealer, the dealers are auto experts, right?

527
00:45:01,056 --> 00:45:14,890
Scott: They could fix your tires, or your trans suspension, or the engine, it's like, they don't have computer experts at the dealerships with the tools, and knowledge, and service manuals necessary, or anything.

528
00:45:15,451 --> 00:45:15,651
Scott: Right?

529
00:45:15,691 --> 00:45:23,905
Scott: It's like, the only people who could probably fix the problems with those computers are the people who programmed the system, who are probably in Japan, and that's it.

530
00:45:23,926 --> 00:45:26,450
Scott: There's probably no one else on Earth who can fix them.

531
00:45:26,890 --> 00:45:39,410
Scott: And I guess your hope is that they could, they would realize those problems, and then push out, you know, over the air patches somehow, and that would fix it, but other than that, there's really nothing you can do.

532
00:45:40,490 --> 00:45:50,603
Rym: Yep, there's no logs, there's no editing access, there's no troubleshooting, and even if you hack, like, I can get into the dealer menu, like, because, you know, Google around, you'll find ways to do it.

533
00:45:51,264 --> 00:45:55,750
Rym: It doesn't give you any troubleshooting tools that will tell you anything.

534
00:45:58,071 --> 00:46:07,050
Scott: Alright, and so, it's, you know, this is very similar to the smart TV situation, where there's a built-in OS that sucks, you have no control over, you know, if it broke, no one could really fix it.

535
00:46:07,150 --> 00:46:29,337
Scott: But, even on a smart TV, now having a smart TV, you can just disable and ignore pretty much all of that, and use something, you know, I could use the Apple TV, I could use, I could just watch channels with the antenna, I don't actually need to use any of the smart TV functions ever, I could ignore it, block it, and disable it to a great extent, never see the ads it's trying to show, because they're

536
00:46:29,357 --> 00:46:30,418
Rym: blocked,

537
00:46:30,438 --> 00:46:39,770
Scott: and you're still getting full use of the TV, there is no feature of the TV that is essential, that you're not accessing, because you're not using the OS that came with it.

538
00:46:39,810 --> 00:46:48,090
Scott: But if you have a car, it's like, okay, you can drive the car, and in Rym's case, have HVAC, you know, temperature control, climate control.

539
00:46:48,411 --> 00:46:50,489
Rym: I was lucky, if it was a Tesla, that wouldn't have worked.

540
00:46:51,250 --> 00:46:51,451
Scott: Right.

541
00:46:51,851 --> 00:47:10,323
Scott: But, you know, navigation, and, you know, I guess you can hear, if you're using your phone instead, okay, but the sound system is not working, because the computer sucks, it's like, that is sort of a core function of the car, that is now not working, and you can't do anything about it.

542
00:47:20,550 --> 00:47:24,349
Scott: They're forcing you to bring it in to get it repaired, it's like, no, they can't even repair it.

543
00:47:24,750 --> 00:47:34,530
Scott: It's like, could you imagine, like, my iPhone broke, shit, there's no right to repair, I can't go to a third party, I have to take it to Apple, you bring it to Apple, they're like, yeah, we can't fix that either.

544
00:47:34,950 --> 00:47:37,200
Scott: It's like, buy a whole new car, just because the radio's not working.

545
00:47:39,771 --> 00:47:41,789
Rym: And three weeks, and we might be able to fix it.

546
00:47:42,351 --> 00:47:55,610
Scott: Right, it's like, buy a whole new car, because the radio's not working, or swap out the whole computer in the car, because the mechanic can't possibly, like, you know, flash the OS, or actually investigate it in any way as a computer expert.

547
00:47:56,591 --> 00:48:05,290
Rym: And the funniest part is, some of the problems are caused by the fact that all these systems have basic interfaces, because everyone has either an Android phone or an iOS phone.

548
00:48:05,911 --> 00:48:13,270
Rym: So as a result, Android and iOS each have a "I am talking to a car" feature, that is designed to standardize this.

549
00:48:13,270 --> 00:48:16,090
Scott: The carplay, or whatever Google calls theirs, they're the same thing.

550
00:48:16,270 --> 00:48:25,750
Rym: The problem is, even if that software works, Apple and Android both update that software, but the car people don't update theirs, nor do they even do a lot of QA.

551
00:48:26,570 --> 00:48:31,750
Scott: Right, I mean, this is one of the reasons I believe Apple was trying to make a car, they failed.

552
00:48:32,990 --> 00:48:41,470
Scott: But the one advantage would be that, like, yeah, if they had made a car, then this aspect would have been more acceptable.

553
00:48:42,111 --> 00:48:50,368
Scott: At least if you had an Apple device of some kind, which I assume you would, because who the fuck buys an Apple car and has Windows computers and Android phones?

554
00:48:50,849 --> 00:48:51,110
Scott: Nobody.

555
00:48:53,272 --> 00:49:07,910
Scott: Apple did abandon their plans for the car, it seems, which probably I'm going to assume for good reasons, but I think there was some partnership with Apple and certain car brands that has come and gone at times.

556
00:49:08,010 --> 00:49:24,510
Scott: I think one car brand, I don't forget, recently got rid of it, but I think it would behoove them to either just stop trying to make a fancy car that didn't work, just make a normal car, and do the computer part right, and that will have appeal on its own.

557
00:49:24,590 --> 00:49:32,530
Scott: You don't need to go wild with some nonsense, and find a car brand that will, you know, cooperate with you.

558
00:49:32,570 --> 00:49:35,983
Rym: Harder with you and make a car that just works.

559
00:49:36,063 --> 00:49:37,709
Rym: the way Apple phones just work.

560
00:49:38,690 --> 00:49:40,889
Scott: I walk in, I set my Apple phone down, and then it just works.

561
00:49:41,070 --> 00:49:45,629
Scott: I'm not trying to Apple fanboy here, because I got an iPhone and all Mac devices right now.

562
00:49:46,311 --> 00:49:47,352
Scott: Google, do the same thing.

563
00:49:47,612 --> 00:50:00,030
Scott: Google, go find, like Google and Samsung, right, it's like, go ahead, find a car manufacturer that, you know, you can make a deal with, and be like, listen, we're going to handle the OS for your car.

564
00:50:00,631 --> 00:50:02,175
Scott: You can stop doing it, right?

565
00:50:02,235 --> 00:50:04,460
Scott: We're going to do it, and that will help you.

566
00:50:04,500 --> 00:50:05,342
Scott: just let us do that.

567
00:50:05,382 --> 00:50:07,427
Scott: That'll save you money on development.

568
00:50:07,488 --> 00:50:08,350
Scott: You won't have to do that.

569
00:50:09,091 --> 00:50:18,324
Scott: And two, you will, and then make money, because people will out there will be like, oh, that car has a computer system that doesn't fucking suck.

570
00:50:18,424 --> 00:50:22,170
Scott: Google made it, and Google keeps updating it, and we can trust it a little bit more.

571
00:50:22,771 --> 00:50:25,138
Scott: And Google, how are they going to make money on this?

572
00:50:25,379 --> 00:50:27,225
Scott: Of course, collecting all your data from your car.

573
00:50:27,706 --> 00:50:28,750
Rym: Come on, come on.

574
00:50:28,990 --> 00:50:34,390
Rym: And then Apple will make money by also doing that, but with a veneer of privacy, and charging the company money.

575
00:50:34,550 --> 00:50:41,670
Scott: Apple will make money on it, because it will have very limited features unless you pay the subscription fee to CarCloud.

576
00:50:42,170 --> 00:50:42,290
Rym: Yep.

577
00:50:43,792 --> 00:51:01,210
Rym: But I think these are, I would say that sound system is a necessity, and it's dangerous to not have one for people who drive a lot, because one of the main reasons to have a sound system is to put on music or some sort of content to keep yourself awake if you're the kind of person who has to drive a long road trip by yourself.

578
00:51:02,073 --> 00:51:06,430
Scott: Yeah, I guess so, but I think even more important is simply just traffic and weather information.

579
00:51:07,211 --> 00:51:07,809
Rym: Yep, navigating.

580
00:51:08,930 --> 00:51:09,210
Scott: Yes.

581
00:51:09,731 --> 00:51:31,110
Scott: So, I think that I agree, there should be, just like a cell phone that has no service can still call 911, I believe there should be some regulation that requires cars, no matter how busted the computer part is, there should be some AM radio functionality, there should be the base set of functionality that should always work.

582
00:51:31,391 --> 00:51:37,230
Scott: Even if the computer dash thing is like fried, like on fire magic smoke came out.

583
00:51:38,390 --> 00:51:40,330
Scott: And the rest, assuming the rest of the car is still just fine.

584
00:51:40,370 --> 00:51:47,170
Rym: Because I mean, I drove a car six miles without a serpentine belt not that long ago, like cars are pretty durable.

585
00:51:48,130 --> 00:51:48,810
Scott: Yeah, somehow.

586
00:51:50,091 --> 00:51:51,709
Rym: I cannot believe I drove that far.

587
00:51:55,600 --> 00:51:57,970
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

588
00:51:58,030 --> 00:52:00,540
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

589
00:52:00,741 --> 00:52:06,760
Scott: Be sure to visit our website at FrontRowClue.com for show notes, discussion, links, and more.

590
